# **`npm ci` fails without this, so a fresh clone cannot install the client.**
#
# `eslint-plugin-react` publishes no release for eslint 10 -- 7.37.5, still the
# latest, peers on `^9.7` -- and holding eslint back a major version for one
# plugin was rejected in `eslint.config.js`, where the whole argument is
# written down. That decision was only ever carried by whoever remembered to
# type the flag: every existing worktree had a `node_modules` predating the
# eslint 10 bump, so nobody hit it until installing from scratch, which is the
# most expensive moment to hit it.
#
# **It weakens every future peer check, which is the cost.** npm will accept
# any conflicting peer here, not only this one -- so
# `ui/src/eslintPeers.test.ts` fails the day the plugin gains an eslint 10
# range, which is the reminder to delete this file.
#
# **At the root since the move to workspaces, and that widened it.** npm reads
# this file from the directory it installs in, and a workspace install runs
# here -- a copy left in `ui/` would be read by nobody and the install would
# fail with ERESOLVE. So the weakening now covers `server/` too, which had no
# peer conflicts of its own to lose.
legacy-peer-deps=true
